Subject: Ask for a timing policy before retiming an existing project

Freecut MIT adaptation (FL-94). Matching a project to its first video,
or any other frame-rate change, used to rewrite the rate while every
clip, keyframe and marker kept its frame number, so an existing edit
silently played faster or slower. When the main timeline has content,
the person now chooses:

- keep-time: timeline frames move to the new rate exactly (integer
  ratio, nearest frame, clip ends rounded with their starts so butted
  clips stay butted); media that read their source at the timeline rate
  are pinned to the old rate so they show the same pictures.
- keep-frames: the previous behaviour, now an explicit choice.

commitProjectMetadataChange refuses a rate change on content without a
policy, so every caller is covered. Undo restores the whole timeline in
one step. Colour needs no prompt: the working range follows the media
(FL-97 owner decision, patch 0033).

